<p>UNO is one of the easiest card games in the world to learn and one of the hardest to put down. If you have never played — or you are about to teach someone who hasn't — this guide walks through a complete first game with nothing assumed. By the end you will know how to deal, what to do on your turn, what every card does, and how to win. It takes about five minutes to read and roughly one round to feel like you have played for years.</p>

<h2>What You Need to Play</h2>
<p>All you need is an UNO deck and two to ten players. Playing online removes even that — there is no deck to buy, shuffle, or lose cards from, and you can start a <a href="/play">free game</a> in seconds. The goal is simple: be the first player to get rid of all your cards. That's the whole game. Everything else is just how you get there.</p>

<h2>Setting Up the Game</h2>
<p>Each player is dealt <strong>seven cards</strong>. The rest of the deck becomes the draw pile, placed face down. The top card of the draw pile is flipped over to start the <strong>discard pile</strong> — this is the card the first player has to match. If that starting card happens to be an action card, most rule sets simply treat it as a normal card for the opening play, though some apply its effect to the first player. Online, the game handles all of this for you automatically, so you just look at your hand and take your turn.</p>

<h2>Taking Your Turn: Match Color, Number, or Symbol</h2>
<p>On your turn, you play one card that <strong>matches the top card of the discard pile</strong> in at least one of three ways:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Color</strong> — red, yellow, green, or blue. A red card can be played on any other red card.</li>
  <li><strong>Number</strong> — a 7 can be played on any other 7, regardless of color.</li>
  <li><strong>Symbol</strong> — a Skip can be played on any other Skip, and so on.</li>
</ul>
<p>So if the top card is a <em>red 6</em>, you could play any red card, any color 6, or a Wild card. If you can't play anything, you <strong>draw one card</strong> from the draw pile. If that card can be played, you may play it right away; if not, your turn ends and play moves on. That single "match one of three things, or draw" loop is the entire core of UNO — the rest is the special cards that make it interesting.</p>

<h2>The Action Cards</h2>
<p>Alongside the plain number cards (0 through 9), each color has three action cards, and there are two kinds of wild card. Here is what each one does:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Skip</strong> — the next player loses their turn. Play jumps right over them.</li>
  <li><strong>Reverse</strong> — the direction of play flips. If it was going clockwise, it now goes counter-clockwise. (In a two-player game, Reverse works like a Skip.)</li>
  <li><strong>Draw Two (+2)</strong> — the next player draws two cards and loses their turn.</li>
  <li><strong>Wild</strong> — play it on anything, then choose which color continues. A great card for when nothing in your hand matches.</li>
  <li><strong>Wild Draw Four (+4)</strong> — the most powerful card. Choose the new color <em>and</em> the next player draws four cards and is skipped. Officially, you're only meant to play it when you have no other card matching the current color.</li>
</ul>
<p>For a deeper breakdown of how many of each card exist and why that matters, see our guide to <a href="/blog/what-uno-cards-mean">what every UNO card does</a>.</p>

<h2>Calling "UNO"</h2>
<p>Here is the rule everyone remembers. When you play your <strong>second-to-last card</strong> — leaving you with just one — you must announce "UNO!" It's a warning to the table that you're about to win. If another player catches you failing to call it before the next player takes their turn, you draw penalty cards (two, in the standard rule). Playing online, there's a short grace window to make the call, and opponents can tap to catch you — so get in the habit of calling UNO in the same motion as playing your card. Forgetting is the most common way beginners hand back a winning position.</p>

<h2>Winning a Round</h2>
<p>The first player to play their very last card wins the round. If you're playing a single round for fun, that's it — that player wins, celebrate, and deal again. If you're keeping score across several rounds, the winner collects points based on the cards <em>still in everyone else's hands</em>: number cards are worth their face value, action cards are worth 20 each, and wild cards are worth 50. The first player to reach 500 points across rounds wins the whole game. Most casual and online games skip scoring entirely and just play round by round.</p>

<h2>A Few Beginner Tips</h2>
<p>You'll pick up strategy naturally, but three habits help right away:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Play your high number cards early.</strong> If a round ever gets scored, you don't want to be caught holding 9s — and high cards tend to be the ones that leave you unable to match.</li>
  <li><strong>Save your Wild cards</strong> for when you're genuinely stuck or about to win, rather than spending them the first time you can't match.</li>
  <li><strong>Watch who's close to winning.</strong> When the player to your left is down to one or two cards, that's the moment to drop a Skip or Draw Two on them.</li>
</ul>
<p>When you're ready for more, our guide to <a href="/blog/how-to-win-at-uno">how to win at UNO</a> covers a dozen tactics, and <a href="/blog/common-uno-mistakes">the most common beginner mistakes</a> shows you what to stop doing.</p>

<h2>House Rules: Why No Two Families Play the Same</h2>
<p>Once you know the basics, you'll discover that almost everyone plays with a few "house rules" they swear are official. Stacking Draw Twos so the penalty piles onto the next player, the Seven-Zero swap, Jump-In — none of these are in the official rulebook, but they're wildly popular. Part of the fun of playing online is that you can turn these on or off deliberately instead of arguing about them: browse our <a href="/house-rules">house rules guide</a> to see what each one does, or just start in <a href="/game-modes">Classic mode</a> for the pure, official experience while you're learning.</p>

<p>That's genuinely everything you need to play your first game. The rules fit on a postcard; the fun comes from playing them. Grab a few friends, deal seven cards each, and go.</p>

